15.6. Riemann's Differential Equation
The hypergeometric differential equation 15.5.1 with the (regular) singular points 0, 1, \infty is a special case of Riemann's differential equation with three (regular) singular points a, b, c
15.6.1 \[\frac{d^{q_{w}}}{dz^{2}}+\left[\frac{1-\alpha-\alpha^{r}}{z-a}+\frac{1-\beta-\beta^{r}}{z-b}+\frac{1-\gamma-\gamma^{r}}{z-c}\right]\frac{dw}{dz}\]
+\left[\frac{\alpha\alpha^{r}(a-b)(a-c)}{z-a}+\frac{\beta\beta^{r}(b-c)(b-a)}{z-b}\right.\]
The pairs of the exponents with respect to the singular points a; b; c are a, a'; b, b'; \(\gamma,\gamma^{r}\) respectively subject to the condition
15.6.2 \alpha+\alpha^{r}+\beta+\beta^{r}+\gamma+\gamma^{r}=1
